:18.0,"off_snaps":58,"epa_per_play":-0.09799,"pass_rate":0.4828,"pass_rate_neutral":0.4706,"def_epa_per_play":-0.04144},{"week":16,"opponent":"SF","home":true,"pf":27.0,"pa":48.0,"off_snaps":58,"epa_per_play":-0.03678,"pass_rate":0.6552,"pass_rate_neutral":0.75,"def_epa_per_play":0.4027},{"week":17,"opponent":"JAX","home":true,"pf":17.0,"pa":23.0,"off_snaps":54,"epa_per_play":-0.2056,"pass_rate":0.6111,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5625,"def_epa_per_play":0.005854},{"week":18,"opponent":"HOU","home":false,"pf":30.0,"pa":38.0,"off_snaps":64,"epa_per_play":-0.07294,"pass_rate":0.5625,"pass_rate_neutral":0.5833,"def_epa_per_play":-0.0189}],"comps":{"offense":[{"season":2023,"team":"TB","distance":0.4986,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Dave Canales"},{"season":2024,"team":"NYJ","distance":0.5728,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2023,"team":"JAX","distance":0.5881,"shared":22,"play_caller":null},{"season":2025,"team":"LV","distance":0.616,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Chip Kelly"},{"season":2025,"team":"PHI","distance":0.6178,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Kevin Patullo"}],"defense":[{"season":2022,"team":"CIN","distance":0.5888,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2018,"team":"NO","distance":0.5989,"shared":18,"play_caller":"Dennis Allen"},{"season":2024,"team":"CIN","distance":0.6401,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Lou Anarumo"},{"season":2023,"team":"LAC","distance":0.6463,"shared":22,"play_caller":"Brandon Staley"},{"season":2023,"team":"PHI","distance":0.6854,"shared":22,"play_caller":null}]},"overview":{"overview":"The Colts went 8-9 in 2025 and outscored opponents 466 to 412. The offense ranked 9th in EPA per play and the defense 16th in EPA allowed, so neither side stood out much. Their most distinctive traits were the 3rd highest play-action rate and the fewest linebackers on the field in the league.","offense":"The Colts offense was well above average by EPA per play, and it was much better running the ball than passing it. Their run and pass mix stayed close to what game situations called for. Their biggest outliers in how they lined up were the 3rd highest play-action rate in the league. Daniel Jones ran the passing game, with Tyler Warren leading the team in targets.","defense":"The Colts defense was about average by EPA allowed per play. It was much better against the run than the pass, ranking 8th and 19th. Kwity Paye was as productive as expected, but the bigger surprise was Adetomiwa Adebawore, who had 4 sacks despite playing only 46% of the snaps. Schematically, they had the fewest linebackers on the field in the league.","special":"The Colts were about average in how often they went for it on fourth down. Their kickers made 94% of field goals, which was one of the best in the league. On the roster side, they put an unusually large share of their salary cap into the secondary, about 26% of the money on the books.","players":"Daniel Jones played nearly every snap at quarterback and threw for 3,101 yards. Targets were spread around, with Tyler Warren and Michael Pittman leading a group where nobody took more than a fifth of the looks. Jonathan Taylor handled most of the work in the backfield with 84% of the carries. On defense, Laiatu Latu led the team with 8.5 sacks and Camryn Bynum picked off 4 passes.","coaching":"Shane Steichen called the offense as head coach and Lou Anarumo called the defense. Their offense looked most like the 2023 Buccaneers under Dave Canales and the 2024 Jets. Their defense looked most like the 2022 Bengals under Lou Anarumo and the 2018 Saints under Dennis Allen.","trends":"The offense went from 24th in EPA per play in 2024 to 9th in 2025, a big step forward. The defense moved from 13th in EPA allowed per play in 2024 to 16th in 2025, a modest decline. The biggest change in approach was base defense usage, which went from 31% to 18%.","notes":"Every data source is available for 2025. This season uses the newer formation labels, which group singleback, I formation and empty sets together as under center, so those older formation rates are not shown."}}